#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# dependencies = [
#     "capabilities-contract>=0.47,<1",
#     "psycopg[binary]>=3.2",
#     "httpx>=0.27",
#     "telethon==1.45.0",
#     "callva-harness-runner==0.12.1",
# ]
# ///
"""telegrambot — an agent's own Telegram channel, through a bot.

Agent-invoked. One connection is one bot, made with BotFather. New messages
arrive and every write leaves through the HTTP Bot API; history is read back
from Telegram by message number through a separate MTProto session the bot's
own token signs in (no account, no login ceremony). Nothing keeps a copy of the
conversation: Telegram is its only home. Run `telegrambot doctor` first.

It refuses: reading chats the bot is not in, searching history, jumping to a
date, listing chats (Telegram gives a bot none of these; `seen` lists what the
bot itself has seen), reading history in a group where the bot does not see
every message (privacy mode on and not an admin), calls, and a second account.

CONNECTIONS
  One connection is one bot and holds everything that bot needs, so it is whole
  by itself on any machine. Where this project keeps it, `telegrambot
  connections` says:

    {"default": "<id>",
     "connections": {"<id>": {
        "secret_env":   "TELEGRAMBOT_<ID>_BOT_TOKEN",
        "api_id_env":   "TELEGRAMBOT_<ID>_API_ID",
        "api_hash_env": "TELEGRAMBOT_<ID>_API_HASH",
        "deepgram_env": "TELEGRAMBOT_<ID>_DEEPGRAM_KEY"}}}

  secret_env    the bot token from BotFather. Required.
  api_id_env    the App api_id from my.telegram.org/apps. With api_hash_env it
                lets the bot read history; without the two the bot runs with
                no history and says so in doctor and in every turn.
  api_hash_env  the App api_hash paired with that api_id.
  deepgram_env  the Deepgram key voice messages are transcribed with. Without
                it voice messages reach a turn as a voice attachment only.

  Each field names an env key; its value resolves through the credential
  cascade (`Where a secret resolves`, at the end of this help), the agent's
  vault first. Give a value and it is sealed into the vault under the
  connection's own key; name a key the vault (or any tier) already holds and
  the connection refers to it instead:

    telegrambot connection add <id> --stdin \
        <<< '{"secret_env": "<token>", "api_id_env": "<api_id>",
              "api_hash_env": "<api_hash>"}'
    telegrambot connection add <id> --env secret_env=<KEY> \
        --env api_id_env=<KEY> --env api_hash_env=<KEY> \
        --env deepgram_env=DEEPGRAM_API_KEY

  A new connection that names no Deepgram key is given a reference to the
  machine's shared one (DEEPGRAM_API_KEY, `telegrambot setup`) where the
  machine holds one, sealed into the vault and named in the answer's
  `prefilled`. That is the only default: a connection that names no key has
  none, and no machine value stands in for it at run time.

  Writes are refused by default: send and react need `allow_write: true`
  granted on the connection. Selection: --connection <id> anywhere on the
  line, else the "default" pointer, else the sole entry.

  One token is polled by one process. A bot another product also polls (an
  app's notification channel, a webhook) cannot be served here: doctor names
  the webhook, and a poll that collides fails `poll_conflict`.

COMMANDS
  Readiness
    doctor [--connection <id>]
        Per connection, from Telegram: the bot (getMe: id, username, name);
        privacy mode (on means a group delivers only commands, replies and
        mentions, unless the bot is an admin there); whether a webhook is set
        (then nothing may poll the bot); history readiness (api_id and
        api_hash resolve and the MTProto session signs in with the token);
        voice readiness (the Deepgram key resolves and Deepgram accepts it).
        Bot-to-Bot mode is not readable: it is reported unknown. ok is the
        token's verdict; what the bot lacks is listed under `missing` and in
        each part's `reason`. Exit 2 token missing or rejected, 5 unreachable.
    connections
        Where every value of every connection resolves from, secrets masked.
        Local only.

  Who the bot has seen
    seen [--kind chat|topic|person]
        The chats, forum topics and people the bot has seen, from the store:
        Telegram id, title or name, username, chat type, whether a person is
        a bot, the bot's own membership in a chat, first and last seen. No
        text. A bot cannot list its chats or find a person by @username, so
        this is how a chat or a person is found to be allowed. Decide by id:
        a name can be anyone's. Local to the store: no Telegram call.

  History (needs api_id and api_hash on the connection)
    history <chat> [--topic N] [--before ID] [--limit N]
        Messages of one chat (and one forum topic) numbered below ID, oldest
        first: steps back from ID through the bot's message numbers, keeping
        only this chat's, at most 5 requests of 100 numbers (about 0.1 s
        each). --limit 1-100 (default 40). Without --before, from the newest
        message the service recorded in that chat. In a private chat the
        numbers are shared with every other private chat of the bot, so a
        busy bot reaches back less far for the same --limit. The answer says
        how many it read of how many it wanted and why it stopped short
        (`complete`, `reason`); an empty answer is a read that found nothing,
        never a refusal. Refused (exit 4 group_not_visible) in a group where
        the bot does not see every message. Exit 7 history_unavailable when
        the connection names no api_id and api_hash.
    download <chat> <message> [--out PATH]
        Fetch the file a message carries (any size) to PATH, default
        <state>/<connection>/files/<chat>-<message>-<name>. {path, bytes}.

  Writes (allow_write on the connection)
    send <chat> <text|-> [--topic N] [--reply-to ID] [--file PATH]
        Send text (- reads it from stdin) as written, with no formatting, cut
        into messages of at most 4096 characters; with --file, the file as a
        document (at most 50 MB) with the text as its caption. {message_ids}.
        Not idempotent: Telegram has no dedup key. A send whose outcome is
        unknown (a timeout after it left) exits 5 delivery_unknown: read the
        chat before sending again.
    react <chat> <message> [<emoji>]
        Set the bot's one reaction on a message; without an emoji, clear it.
        Repeating it is safe. The emoji must be one the chat allows.

  Prompts (the agent's own text over the standard the bundle ships)
    prompts list
    prompts show <key>
    prompts put <key> [--base ID] [--note TEXT]     (body on stdin)
    prompts history <key>
    prompts retire <key>
        Keys: bot (the agent's one prompt, read every turn after the
        standard; empty is the standard alone), delegation (replaces the
        standard text about handing work to a background job), and
        context.<name> (a chat's or a topic's own context, which the service
        settings name). Every save is a new version; the one it replaces
        stays in history. --base names the version you read (its id or six
        or more of its first characters) and refuses the save (exit 6
        base_mismatch) when another was saved since. retire leaves no active
        version and deletes nothing. show answers {key, body, version,
        source}: store, bundle (delegation's standard text) or none.

  Profiles (how a turn runs: harness, model, effort, rights)
    profiles list | profiles show <name>
    profiles save <name> <file|-> [--base VERSION]
        Every telegrambot profile this project reaches: the project's version
        in the store, the global one, then the bundled telegrambot-claude,
        -codex, -job-claude, -job-codex and the reply gate's -gate-claude and
        -gate-codex. A name always starts telegrambot-. save writes a new
        version at this project's scope (exit 6 conflict when --base is not
        the active one); inside a turn only for a requester whose role may
        /set. list and show never print an env_set value or a settings object.

  Jobs (work a turn hands off; needs `delegation: allowed`)
    jobs help | list | active | show | register | amend | submit | stop |
    resume | discard
        `telegrambot jobs help` prints the whole surface. Inside a turn the
        worker wrapper fixes the chat, topic, requester, origin message and
        profile, and this CLI takes them from the turn again.

  Service
    service run [--machine]        foreground; the supervisor's command
    service stop|reload [--machine] [--timeout S]
    service status|logs|doctor [--machine]   (logs: --tail N)
    service harness [--json]       the harnesses this project's turns run on

SERVICE
  One process per machine serves every bot of every project that joined it
  (`service run --machine`); `service run` in a project not joined serves
  that project alone with the same code. A project joins by being placed on
  the node in mode shared (`capabilities service start telegrambot`, or
  `capabilities place telegrambot --node <node> --mode shared`); the node
  manager writes the list, ~/.config/telegrambot/service/projects.json, and
  this CLI never does. One lock per project decides which process serves it.
  The service needs the project's store (store_not_configured without one),
  serves every connection the project grants with allow_write (checked again
  every 30 s), and one getUpdates poll per bot: a bot with a webhook, or one
  another process polls, is not served (webhook_set, poll_conflict).

  A message is answered in this order; the first stop wins: the chat is
  allowed (a private chat by direct_messages.mode and allowed_users, a group
  by allowed_groups) -> /start is never a turn -> a last line #noreply ->
  the sender may address it (may_address) -> other bots: at most
  agent_dialogue.max_turns turns in a row (default 4; a person's message
  starts the count again; Telegram's own is_bot says who is a bot) -> a
  service command -> in a group, addressed: @username, an alias, a reply to
  the bot (`triggers` switches each off; require_reference false admits
  every message), else the reply gate, else an ambient voice message in an
  `auto` chat. A stranger is ignored silently and recorded in `seen`.

  An admitted message is reserved in the store before anything is done, the
  chat shows the bot typing at once, and one person's messages within the
  channel's debounce become one turn. A channel is a chat or one forum topic
  (General is topic 1); one turn runs per channel at a time. A turn reads the
  conversation back by message number (tail_size, at most history_requests
  reads), climbs the reply chain of the first message, and runs in a fresh
  harness session on the channel's profile; its answer, cut at
  `=== REPLY ===`, is sent in parts of 4096, as a reply to the request in a
  group. The answer, its progress lines, what it sends and a job's result
  are rendered from Markdown into Telegram's own formatting (bold, italic,
  strikethrough, code, code blocks, links, quotes, lists; a heading becomes
  a bold line and a table a code block), sent as message entities with no
  parse mode; a part whose formatting Telegram refuses is sent again as
  plain text. An empty answer is silence. A request ending in #external has
  everything sent for it end in #noreply. In a group where the bot does not
  see every message (privacy on, not an admin) no history is read and the
  bot answers only when addressed; with no api_id and api_hash on the
  connection no history is read anywhere. Each turn's channel state says
  which, and how far its read reached.

  The prompt, top down: the bundle's mechanics (read every turn, never cut),
  the bundle's behaviour (`prompt_mode: replace` drops it), the agent's own
  prompt (`prompts put bot`), the delegation text where the chat may hand
  off work, the group's or direct sender's context, the topic's context,
  then the channel state, the request and the conversation. context_mode
  exclusive on a group cuts the behaviour and the agent's prompt; on a topic
  it cuts the group's context too. A job reads the job mechanics, the job
  behaviour, the agent's prompt, its state, its brief and the conversation.

  Voice: an addressed voice message (every one in a voice_transcription
  `auto` chat) is fetched, transcribed with the connection's Deepgram key,
  kept on disk under the connection's state, and echoed into the chat as a
  quote when voice_echo is on; an ambient one is answered only when its
  words name the bot. The turn reads it as `[voice] <text>`.

  Commands in a chat, each gated by control.roles: /status (settings,
  profile, history, the replies in flight numbered, open jobs), /stop
  (everything in flight here) and /stop N (one of the last /status list),
  /profiles, /reload, /set tail|debounce|worker-timeout|profile|
  voice-transcription|voice-echo <value|default>, /help. A command with
  another bot's @suffix is not read at all. The bot's command menu is set at
  start. Roles come from allowed_users.<id>.role, a group member's role,
  member_role, direct_messages.default_role; authority.roles hands each turn
  its CAPABILITIES_AUTH_CONTEXT, and roles.<role>.profile names the profile
  the sender's turn runs on, over the chat's, the topic's and /set profile.

  The reply gate (allowed_groups.<id>.gate {enabled, mode shadow|live,
  brief, context_messages, context_chars, max_batch, model, effort}): in a
  group the bot sees whole, unaddressed messages of people collect per
  channel and, after the debounce, one light run on telegrambot-gate-<the
  channel's harness> decides ignore, react with one emoji, or reply. shadow
  logs only; live acts. A failing run is named by service doctor
  (gate_failing).

  Inside a turn or a job the worker wrapper (service/worker-bin) is first on
  PATH: `telegrambot progress <text>`, `send current <text> [--file PATH]`,
  `history`, `download <message>`, `react <message> [<emoji>]` and `jobs`
  act on this chat only; the channel state names each command.

SETTINGS
  `telegrambot settings` reads and writes them (service/settings.json at the
  project's scope over the global one; an agent in the store keeps them as
  rows). The surface is closed: an unknown key is refused with its path.
    assistant_name, prompt_mode (extend|replace)
    direct_messages {mode: allowed_users|anyone|off, default_role}
    allowed_users {<user id>: {name, role, may_address, profile,
      worker_timeout, context, context_prompt, context_mode, project,
      voice_transcription, voice_echo, delegation}}
    allowed_groups {<chat id>: {name, member_role, members {<user id>:
      {name, role, may_address}}, may_address (anyone|members), aliases,
      require_reference, triggers, gate, profile, worker_timeout,
      voice_transcription, voice_echo, context, context_prompt,
      context_mode, project, delegation, agent_dialogue {max_turns},
      topics {<topic id>: {name, profile, context, context_prompt,
      context_mode, project}}}}
    agent_dialogue {max_turns}, roles {<role>: {profile}},
    control {roles {<role>: {commands}}},
    authority {default, roles {<role>: {allowed_capabilities}}}
    defaults {tail_size 40, history_requests 5, debounce 2, worker_timeout
      120, progress_after 15, progress_from_stream false, max_parallel_jobs
      1, job_recovery requeue|inspect, profile, job_profile,
      voice_transcription addressed, voice_echo true, delegation disabled}
  context is inline text; context_prompt names the prompt key
  context.<name>. project runs that chat's (or topic's, or person's) turns
  in another project folder. A profile left unset is telegrambot-claude
  where claude is on PATH, else telegrambot-codex. Settings, connections and
  profiles take `service reload`; prompts are read every turn.

CHATS AND TOPICS
  A chat is its Bot API id: a person's id for a private chat, -<id> for a
  basic group, -100<id> for a supergroup. A forum topic is its number (the
  message that opened it); General is 1. `seen` lists both.

CONTRACT VERBS
  telegrambot help | stub | manifest --json | guide [<topic>]
  telegrambot connection add|remove|seal ... | setup [set|unset]
  telegrambot settings [get|set|unset|put|export|edit] ...
  telegrambot ids list|get <l>|set <l> <v> [--note <t>]|rm <l>
  telegrambot refs | inventory | migrate [status]

I/O
  Success is JSON on stdout; failure is {"error": {"code", "message", "hint"?,
  "status"?}} on stderr.

EXIT CODES
  0 success · 2 auth (token missing or rejected, api_id/api_hash rejected) ·
  3 not found (unknown chat or message) · 4 policy (the project gate, a write
  on a connection without a write grant, the read-only switch, history in a
  group the bot does not fully see - ask the user, never lift a gate
  yourself) · 5 server/network (timeout, 5xx, 429 after retries, a flood
  wait, delivery_unknown, poll_conflict) · 6 input (bad arguments, a request
  Telegram refused) · 7 history_unavailable (the connection names no api_id
  and api_hash), or service doctor found the service unable to serve, not
  running or not current · 8 another process already runs the service here.
"""

